// The same rule at a different door. Six places introduce a name - a
// declaration, a global, a parameter, an assignment, a loop counter and a
// multi-assign target - and a guard on five of them is a rule with a hole.
//
// The clause sits BELOW the loop on purpose: `uses` is per file, not per
// line, and the parser has collected every borrow before the checker starts.
// So the position of the clause cannot matter, and this is what says so.
fun <> = main() {
  for fmod : 1 to 2 {
    ? fmod
  }
}

uses fmod
